syntax: parse zsh's alternate forms of loops and if, and repeat

Zsh documents alternate forms for its complex commands (zshmisc(1),
"ALTERNATE FORMS FOR COMPLEX COMMANDS") which the parser rejected:

	for f (*.txt) echo $f
	for f (*.txt); do echo $f; done
	for k v in a 1 b 2; do echo $k $v; done
	for f in a b; echo $f
	for ((i = 0; i < 3; i++)) echo $i
	foreach f (a b c) echo $f; end
	if [[ -n $x ]] { echo yes } elif [[ -n $y ]] echo maybe
	while (( i < 3 )) { (( i++ )) }
	until [[ -f x ]] sleep 1
	select x (a b) { break }
	repeat 3 echo hi

Reproducer, before this change:

	$ printf 'for f (*.txt) echo $f\n' | shfmt -ln zsh
	<standard input>:1:1: `for foo` must be followed by `in`, `do`, `;`, or a newline
	$ printf 'if [[ -n $x ]] { echo yes }\n' | shfmt -ln zsh
	<standard input>:1:16: statements must be separated by &, ; or a newline

and `repeat 3 echo hi` parsed as a call to a command named repeat.

With LangZsh, the condition lists of if, elif, while and until now end at
a statement which is not followed by a separator, so that a body in braces
or a single sublist can follow. IfClause, WhileClause and ForClause gain
Braces and Short fields (ForClause already had Braces), ForClause gains
Foreach, WordIter gains Names for loops over many names and Lparen/Rparen
for the parenthesized word list, and the new RepeatClause node represents
repeat loops. The printer writes all of them in their standard
then/fi and do/done forms, just like it already did for bash's for loops
with braces.

Part of https://github.com/mvdan/sh/issues/120.

